[T] For a matrix program, the Gram–Schmidt process works better with orthonormal vectors. Starting with $\textbf{x}_{1}$, $\ldots$, $\textbf{x}_{p}$ as in Theorem 11, let $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrr}
      \textbf{x}_{1} & \cdots & \textbf{x}_{p}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Suppose $Q$ is an $n \times k$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis for the subspace $W_{k}$ spanned by the first $k$ columns of $A$. Then for $\textbf{x}$ in $\text{ℝ}^{n}$, $QQ^{T}\textbf{x}$ is the orthogonal projection of $\textbf{x}$ onto $W_{k}$ (Theorem 10 in Section 6.3). If $\textbf{x}_{k + 1}$ is the next column of $A$, then equation (2) in the proof of Theorem 11 becomes $\mathbf{v}_{k + 1} = \mathbf{x}_{k + 1} - Q(Q^{T}\mathbf{x}_{k + 1})$
